🇨🇳 China is jamming Starlink over the South China Sea

The Philippine side stated that in the area of the disputed Scarborough Reef (Chinese name Huangyan), China has started interfering not only with ships but also with Manila's patrol aircraft.

The events of February 18-19 showed that the problem is widespread. Starlink systems on board aircraft, which carried out three patrol flights in the area of the reef, were affected. When approaching, a notification appeared on the aircraft's instruments that the Starlink service is "unavailable in this country". The connection was restored only after the aircraft had moved 13, 15 and 17 nautical miles respectively away from the specified area.

These cases indicate systematic actions on the part of the PRC. In fact, it may not just be about jamming the signal, but about creating a so-called "geofence", in which the Huangyan Island area is designated as Chinese territory, which automatically activates a message about the impossibility of providing the service.

It is known that the Chinese government has not issued licenses to SpaceX to operate Starlink on its territory, citing national security considerations and the requirement to route internet traffic through state-controlled gateways.

The Philippines was particularly alarmed by the dynamics of the Starlink signal's appearance at different distances from the reef.

This could mean:

· adjustment of the parameters of the restricted zone;
· testing and calibration of the system;
· gradual expansion of the blocking radius;
· demonstrative consolidation of claims to the disputed area.

The radius is not static - it changes, indicating active management of the process.

What is happening demonstrates a new trend: the struggle for territory is increasingly being transferred to the sphere of technology and information infrastructure. Control over communication, navigation and data transmission is becoming a strategic tool.

Blocking the internet signal during maritime monitoring flights has several consequences for Manila:

· reduction of situational awareness;
· restriction of real-time data transmission;
· complicating the coordination of actions;
· increasing security risks.

